  "priority": 4,
  "slug": "app-07-damage-region-render-optimization",
  "title": "app-07: Damage-region render optimization",
  "discussedAt": "",
  "contextReady": false,
  "contextReadyReason": "",
  "summary": "Ottimizzazione render su scene damage-aware e scissor union.",
  "description": "\n\n═══ DETAIL RESCUED FROM PLAN.md (2026-07-26, before that file was deleted) ═══\nAnalysed 2026-06-22. Re-verify the file references before building.\n\nWHY IT DOES NOT WORK TODAY\n`heca-renderer`'s `composite.rs` keeps a PERSISTENT scene texture precisely so a frame can\nre-render only the changed region (scissored) and let unchanged pixels survive — cheap idle and\nanimation frames (a cursor blink, a hover glow) instead of repainting the whole UI 60 times a\nsecond. That is a real battery and heat win, especially for an idle terminal.\n\nIT IS NOT REALISED: `render_frame` clears the whole scene every frame and redraws every pane in\nfull, so there is nothing preserved to build on.\n\nWHAT WAS REMOVED AND WHY\nThe chrome-level `ChromeDamageMode` (Full / Tracked / ForceFullOnNextRequest) was the app-side gate\nfor this. Layered over an unconditional per-frame clear it only ever DROPPED chrome content, which\nshowed up as a dark \"re-render flash\" on the split button's press animation. Fixed 2026-06-22 by\nmaking chrome always full-repaint (`render.rs`, `damage: None`) and REMOVING the dead\n`ChromeDamageMode` machinery — it was unsound as built. Do not resurrect it as-is.\n\nTHE FOUR STEPS TO ACTUALLY LAND IT\n1. Stop the unconditional per-frame scene clear.\n2. Make PANES damage-aware — only re-render changed pane regions. Chrome alone is not enough.\n3. Scissor the clear and each renderer to the union damage rect against the preserved scene.\n4. Re-introduce an app-side damage gate, designed against the above rather than layered on top.\n\nRenderer-level `GridRenderer::set_damage` and the compositor's preserved scene already exist to\nbuild on.\n\nRISK: rendering correctness — transparent-pane double-blend, stale pixels. GPU ordering is not\nunit-tested, so this MUST be verified by running the app, not by a green suite.\n\nRELATED: the reactive-store work noted that feeding signal changes into the\n`needs_paint`/`collect_damage` path is \"the deep part of the work\". Same problem, same place.\n",
  "notes": "",
